      "updated": 1790600903998,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "                                                                            REPLICATION\nWHAT REPLICATION SOLVES:\n\nSharding solves capacity (too much data for one node). Replication solves availability and read scalability (too many reads for one node, or need \nto survive node failure).\n\nReplication means storing copies of the same data on multiple nodes. If one node fails, others have the data.\n\nTWO REASONS TO REPLICATE:\n\nFault tolerance: If one node dies, the system keeps working using replicas.\nRead scaling: Read traffic can be distributed across replicas, multiplying read capacity.\n\n                                                PRIMARY-REPLICA REPLICATION (LEADER-FOLLOWER)\n                                            \nThe most common replication topology.\n\n                Write\n                  │\n                  ▼\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│      PRIMARY NODE                   │  ← All writes go here\n│   (Leader / Master)                    │\n└─────────────────────────┘\n           │           │\n    replicates     replicates\n           │           │\n           ▼           ▼\n┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐\n│   REPLICA 1         │  │   REPLICA 2       │  ← Reads can be distributed here\n│  (Follower)           │  │  (Follower)           │\n└──────────────┘  └──────────────┘\n\nHow writes propagate:\n\n1. Client writes to the primary\n2. Primary writes to its local storage (WAL — Write-Ahead Log)\n3. Primary sends replication stream to replicas\n4. Replicas apply the changes to their local storage\n5. (Optionally) replicas acknowledge to primary\n6. Primary acknowledges to client\n\nThe timing of step 6 relative to step 5 determines the consistency model:\n\nSYNCHRONOUS REPLICATION\n\nPrimary waits for at least one replica to confirm before acknowledging the write to the client.\n\n- Client → Write → Primary\n- Primary → Write → Primary disk ✅\n- Primary → Replicate → Replica 1\n- Replica 1 → Acknowledge → Primary ✅\n- Primary → \"Write successful\" → Client ✅\n- (Primary may still be replicating to Replica 2 asynchronously)\n\nGuarantees: If primary crashes immediately after acknowledging, Replica 1 has the data. No data loss.\n\nCost: Write latency = max(primary write latency, replica 1 write latency + network round trip). If Replica 1 is in a different datacenter, writes are \nslower.\n\nPostgreSQL synchronous_commit = on: PostgreSQL's synchronous replication mode. Write doesn't return until at least one standby has confirmed.\n\nASYNCHRONOUS REPLICATION\n\nPrimary acknowledges the write immediately after writing to its own disk. Replication to replicas happens in the background.\n\n- Client → Write → Primary\n- Primary → Write → Primary disk ✅\n- Primary → \"Write successful\" → Client ✅  ← immediate response\n- (Primary replicates to replicas later, in background)\n\nGuarantees: Fast writes. But if primary crashes before replicating, the data written since the last replication is lost.\n\nThe replication lag problem: Replicas are always slightly behind the primary. If you read from a replica immediately after writing to the primary, you \nmight read stale data.\n\nClient writes \"email = new@example.com\" to Primary\nClient immediately reads from Replica → gets \"email = old@example.com\"\n(Replica hasn't received the update yet)\n\nThis is replication lag and it's the source of many production bugs. \"Why does my profile show the old email after I just updated it?\" — because \nthe read went to a replica that hasn't caught up yet.\n\nMySQL asynchronous replication: MySQL's default replication mode. Primary doesn't wait for replicas. This is fast but risks data loss and staleness.\n\nSEMI-SYNCHRONOUS REPLICATION\n\nA compromise. Primary waits for at least ONE replica to confirm, but not all of them. The write is durable (at least two copies exist) but latency \nis only the latency to the nearest replica.\n\nThis is MySQL's semi-synchronous replication (rpl_semi_sync_master_enabled). Common in production MySQL deployments.\n\n                                                    FAILOVER - WHAT HAPPENS WHEN PRIMARY DIES\n                                                    \nWhen the primary fails, one of the replicas must be promoted to primary. This process is called failover.\n\nMANUAL FAILOVER: A human decides which replica to promote and updates configuration. Slow (minutes to hours). Simple. No risk of promoting a stale \nreplica by mistake.\n\nAUTOMATIC FAILOVER: The system detects primary failure and automatically promotes a replica. Fast (seconds). Requires careful implementation to avoid split-brain.\n\n\nSPLIT-BRAIN PROBLEM:\nThe most dangerous failure mode in replicated systems.\n\nScenario:\nPrimary (Server A) and Replica (Server B) are connected\nNetwork partition occurs — A and B can't communicate\n\nServer A: \"I can't reach Server B. Server B must be dead. I'll stay as primary.\"\nServer B: \"I can't reach Server A. Server A must be dead. I'll promote myself to primary.\"\n\nNow BOTH A and B accept writes.\nBoth are primary. Both have clients writing to them.\nData diverges. You now have two inconsistent sources of truth.\nWhen the partition heals, you have a conflict with no clear winner.\n\nSOLUTIONS TO SPLIT-BRAIN:\n\nQuorum-based promotion: A replica only promotes itself if a majority of nodes agree the primary is down. With 3 nodes (1 primary + 2 replicas), if \nprimary is unreachable:\n\nBoth replicas can still communicate with each other (2 of 3 nodes = majority)\nThey agree the primary is down → one promotes itself\nThe old primary can't get to a majority (only 1 of 3) → it can't stay primary\nOnly one \"primary\" can achieve majority agreement at any time\n\nSTONITH (Shoot The Other Node In The Head): When a failover is triggered, the system actively shuts down the old primary before promoting a \nnew one. Violent but eliminates split-brain.\n\nFencing tokens: When a replica promotes itself, it gets an incrementing token from ZooKeeper. Any writes from the old primary with a lower token \nnumber are rejected. The old primary becomes ineffective even if it thinks it's still primary.\n\n                                                         MULTI-PRAIMARY REPLICATION (MULTI-LEADER)\n\nMultiple nodes accept writes. Each node replicates to all others.\n\nClient A writes → Primary 1\nClient B writes → Primary 2\n\nPrimary 1 → replicates to → Primary 2\nPrimary 2 → replicates to → Primary 1\n\nWhen two primaries receive conflicting writes for the same record:\n\nPrimary 1 receives: user.name = \"John\"\nPrimary 2 receives simultaneously: user.name = \"Jane\"\nThey replicate to each other\nNow both have a conflict: which name is correct?\n\nThis is the write conflict problem and is the fundamental challenge of multi-primary replication.\n\nCONFLICT RESOLUTION STRATEGIES:\n\nLast Write Wins (LWW): Use the latest timestamp. Simple. Problem: clocks across machines drift. The \"later\" timestamp might actually have been \nwritten first. CRDTs and vector clocks (covered later in this distributed systems section) are better solutions.\n\nCustom resolution: Application-level logic decides how to merge conflicting versions.\n\nAvoid conflicts: Route all writes for the same record to the same primary (using routing or sticky sessions). If User 123's writes always go to \nPrimary 1, no conflicts for User 123's data.\n\nWHEN MULTI-PRIMARY MAKES SENSE:\n\nMultiple datacenters — each datacenter has its own primary for low-latency local writes\nOffline clients — a mobile app is a \"primary\" that accepts writes offline and syncs when connected (like Google Docs offline mode)\nCollaborative editing — all users are primaries writing concurrently (handled with CRDTs)\nLeaderless Replication\n\nNo single node is \"the primary.\" Any node can accept any write. The client writes to multiple nodes directly.\n\nClient → writes to Node A, Node B, Node C simultaneously (or via coordinator)\n         (at least W nodes must confirm the write)\n\nClient reads from Node A, Node B, Node C\n         (at least R nodes must respond, take the most recent value)\n\nThis is the Dynamo model (Amazon's internal system, ancestor of DynamoDB and Cassandra).\n\nQuorum writes: Write to W of N nodes. Write is considered successful when W nodes confirm.\nQuorum reads: Read from R of N nodes. Compare versions, return the most recent.\n\nIf W + R > N, reads and writes overlap — you're guaranteed to read at least one node that has the latest write.\n\nThis is covered in detail in the next section (Quorum Reads/Writes).\n\nREPAIR MECHANISMS:\n\nWhen a node is offline during writes, it misses updates. When it comes back, it's stale. Two mechanisms fix this:\n\nRead repair: When a read reveals a node has stale data (compared to other nodes in the quorum read), the client (or coordinator) sends the co-\nrrect value back to the stale node.\n\nAnti-entropy: Background process that continuously compares nodes and syncs differences. Slower but catches cases read repair misses.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                                                                            REPLICATION\nWHAT REPLICATION SOLVES:\n\nSharding solves capacity (too much data for one node). Replication solves availability and read scalability (too many reads for one node, or need \nto survive node failure).\n\nReplication means storing copies of the same data on multiple nodes. If one node fails, others have the data.\n\nTWO REASONS TO REPLICATE:\n\nFault tolerance: If one node dies, the system keeps working using replicas.\nRead scaling: Read traffic can be distributed across replicas, multiplying read capacity.\n\n                                                PRIMARY-REPLICA REPLICATION (LEADER-FOLLOWER)\n                                            \nThe most common replication topology.\n\n                Write\n                  │\n                  ▼\n┌─────────────────────────┐\n│      PRIMARY NODE                   │  ← All writes go here\n│   (Leader / Master)                    │\n└─────────────────────────┘\n           │           │\n    replicates     replicates\n           │           │\n           ▼           ▼\n┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐\n│   REPLICA 1         │  │   REPLICA 2       │  ← Reads can be distributed here\n│  (Follower)           │  │  (Follower)           │\n└──────────────┘  └──────────────┘\n\nHow writes propagate:\n\n1. Client writes to the primary\n2. Primary writes to its local storage (WAL — Write-Ahead Log)\n3. Primary sends replication stream to replicas\n4. Replicas apply the changes to their local storage\n5. (Optionally) replicas acknowledge to primary\n6. Primary acknowledges to client\n\nThe timing of step 6 relative to step 5 determines the consistency model:\n\nSYNCHRONOUS REPLICATION\n\nPrimary waits for at least one replica to confirm before acknowledging the write to the client.\n\n- Client → Write → Primary\n- Primary → Write → Primary disk ✅\n- Primary → Replicate → Replica 1\n- Replica 1 → Acknowledge → Primary ✅\n- Primary → \"Write successful\" → Client ✅\n- (Primary may still be replicating to Replica 2 asynchronously)\n\nGuarantees: If primary crashes immediately after acknowledging, Replica 1 has the data. No data loss.\n\nCost: Write latency = max(primary write latency, replica 1 write latency + network round trip). If Replica 1 is in a different datacenter, writes are \nslower.\n\nPostgreSQL synchronous_commit = on: PostgreSQL's synchronous replication mode. Write doesn't return until at least one standby has confirmed.\n\nASYNCHRONOUS REPLICATION\n\nPrimary acknowledges the write immediately after writing to its own disk. Replication to replicas happens in the background.\n\n- Client → Write → Primary\n- Primary → Write → Primary disk ✅\n- Primary → \"Write successful\" → Client ✅  ← immediate response\n- (Primary replicates to replicas later, in background)\n\nGuarantees: Fast writes. But if primary crashes before replicating, the data written since the last replication is lost.\n\nThe replication lag problem: Replicas are always slightly behind the primary. If you read from a replica immediately after writing to the primary, you \nmight read stale data.\n\nClient writes \"email = new@example.com\" to Primary\nClient immediately reads from Replica → gets \"email = old@example.com\"\n(Replica hasn't received the update yet)\n\nThis is replication lag and it's the source of many production bugs. \"Why does my profile show the old email after I just updated it?\" — because \nthe read went to a replica that hasn't caught up yet.\n\nMySQL asynchronous replication: MySQL's default replication mode. Primary doesn't wait for replicas. This is fast but risks data loss and staleness.\n\nSEMI-SYNCHRONOUS REPLICATION\n\nA compromise. Primary waits for at least ONE replica to confirm, but not all of them. The write is durable (at least two copies exist) but latency \nis only the latency to the nearest replica.\n\nThis is MySQL's semi-synchronous replication (rpl_semi_sync_master_enabled). Common in production MySQL deployments.\n\n                                                    FAILOVER - WHAT HAPPENS WHEN PRIMARY DIES\n                                                    \nWhen the primary fails, one of the replicas must be promoted to primary. This process is called failover.\n\nMANUAL FAILOVER: A human decides which replica to promote and updates configuration. Slow (minutes to hours). Simple. No risk of promoting a stale \nreplica by mistake.\n\nAUTOMATIC FAILOVER: The system detects primary failure and automatically promotes a replica. Fast (seconds). Requires careful implementation to avoid split-brain.\n\n\nSPLIT-BRAIN PROBLEM:\nThe most dangerous failure mode in replicated systems.\n\nScenario:\nPrimary (Server A) and Replica (Server B) are connected\nNetwork partition occurs — A and B can't communicate\n\nServer A: \"I can't reach Server B. Server B must be dead. I'll stay as primary.\"\nServer B: \"I can't reach Server A. Server A must be dead. I'll promote myself to primary.\"\n\nNow BOTH A and B accept writes.\nBoth are primary. Both have clients writing to them.\nData diverges. You now have two inconsistent sources of truth.\nWhen the partition heals, you have a conflict with no clear winner.\n\nSOLUTIONS TO SPLIT-BRAIN:\n\nQuorum-based promotion: A replica only promotes itself if a majority of nodes agree the primary is down. With 3 nodes (1 primary + 2 replicas), if \nprimary is unreachable:\n\nBoth replicas can still communicate with each other (2 of 3 nodes = majority)\nThey agree the primary is down → one promotes itself\nThe old primary can't get to a majority (only 1 of 3) → it can't stay primary\nOnly one \"primary\" can achieve majority agreement at any time\n\nSTONITH (Shoot The Other Node In The Head): When a failover is triggered, the system actively shuts down the old primary before promoting a \nnew one. Violent but eliminates split-brain.\n\nFencing tokens: When a replica promotes itself, it gets an incrementing token from ZooKeeper. Any writes from the old primary with a lower token \nnumber are rejected. The old primary becomes ineffective even if it thinks it's still primary.\n\n                                                         MULTI-PRAIMARY REPLICATION (MULTI-LEADER)\n\nMultiple nodes accept writes. Each node replicates to all others.\n\nClient A writes → Primary 1\nClient B writes → Primary 2\n\nPrimary 1 → replicates to → Primary 2\nPrimary 2 → replicates to → Primary 1\n\nWhen two primaries receive conflicting writes for the same record:\n\nPrimary 1 receives: user.name = \"John\"\nPrimary 2 receives simultaneously: user.name = \"Jane\"\nThey replicate to each other\nNow both have a conflict: which name is correct?\n\nThis is the write conflict problem and is the fundamental challenge of multi-primary replication.\n\nCONFLICT RESOLUTION STRATEGIES:\n\nLast Write Wins (LWW): Use the latest timestamp. Simple. Problem: clocks across machines drift. The \"later\" timestamp might actually have been \nwritten first. CRDTs and vector clocks (covered later in this distributed systems section) are better solutions.\n\nCustom resolution: Application-level logic decides how to merge conflicting versions.\n\nAvoid conflicts: Route all writes for the same record to the same primary (using routing or sticky sessions). If User 123's writes always go to \nPrimary 1, no conflicts for User 123's data.\n\nWHEN MULTI-PRIMARY MAKES SENSE:\n\nMultiple datacenters — each datacenter has its own primary for low-latency local writes\nOffline clients — a mobile app is a \"primary\" that accepts writes offline and syncs when connected (like Google Docs offline mode)\nCollaborative editing — all users are primaries writing concurrently (handled with CRDTs)\nLeaderless Replication\n\nNo single node is \"the primary.\" Any node can accept any write. The client writes to multiple nodes directly.\n\nClient → writes to Node A, Node B, Node C simultaneously (or via coordinator)\n         (at least W nodes must confirm the write)\n\nClient reads from Node A, Node B, Node C\n         (at least R nodes must respond, take the most recent value)\n\nThis is the Dynamo model (Amazon's internal system, ancestor of DynamoDB and Cassandra).\n\nQuorum writes: Write to W of N nodes. Write is considered successful when W nodes confirm.\nQuorum reads: Read from R of N nodes. Compare versions, return the most recent.\n\nIf W + R > N, reads and writes overlap — you're guaranteed to read at least one node that has the latest write.\n\nThis is covered in detail in the next section (Quorum Reads/Writes).\n\nREPAIR MECHANISMS:\n\nWhen a node is offline during writes, it misses updates. When it comes back, it's stale. Two mechanisms fix this:\n\nRead repair: When a read reveals a node has stale data (compared to other nodes in the quorum read), the client (or coordinator) sends the co-\nrrect value back to the stale node.\n\nAnti-entropy: Background process that continuously compares nodes and syncs differences. Slower but catches cases read repair misses.",
